        customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights1", "taillights2"], drllight: ["drls"] },
        author: "Mercedes Benz CL600 C140 (https://skfb.ly/pvuqL) by Nieve5677 is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
        popupAnimationName: "",
        drivingExperience: "Driving the US Market C140 CL 500 focuses entirely on the endless comfort of American highways. The 5.0-liter M119 V8's 315 horsepower provides abundant power reserves, despite being slightly detuned compared to its European counterpart due to strict emissions regulations. Under acceleration, the car surges forward with dignified, unstoppable momentum, accompanied by silky smooth shifts from the 4-speed hydraulic automatic transmission. The double-glazed windows and massive sound insulation completely isolate you from the outside world; incredible peace reigns inside even at high speeds. While the heavy weight is apparent in corners, in a straight line it is a true asphalt-devouring yacht built to conquer miles of interstate.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The early 5.0-liter M119 V8 destined for the North American market arrived with slightly restricted 315 horsepower output due to stricter emissions standards. This slight reduction is entirely imperceptible during daily driving; the engine still moves the massive coupe with staggering confidence. The mechanical foundation is identical to the European version, meaning you get the legendary, near-indestructible closed-deck aluminum block. Unfortunately, US-market models also received the era's infamous biodegradable engine wiring harness. The heat causes the insulation to crumble into dust, creating short circuits that will instantly fry the expensive LH-Jetronic ECU. Because the EZL ignition module is virtually impossible to source today, preventative replacement of the wiring harness is vital for the car's survival. Mechanical shortcomings include the plastic upper timing chain guides, which inevitably become brittle and break. Broken plastic pieces falling into the timing case will cause the chain to skip, resulting in catastrophic engine failure. Cracked plastic oil tubes under the camshafts lead to oil pressure loss and constant, loud ticking from the hydraulic lifters. The ignition system's dual distributor caps are highly sensitive to moisture, causing severe misfires. The US models' EGR system is also prone to carbon buildup, causing idle fluctuations. The 4-speed hydraulic automatic transmission is bulletproof, assuming fluid changes are never neglected. The incredible curb weight of over 4,500 lbs causes suspension bushings and shock absorbers to wear out at an above-average rate. Inside, A/C evaporator replacement remains a dreaded, multi-day dash-out job. While maintaining it requires significant financial commitment, once the necessary upgrades are performed, this V8 remains a reliable companion for a lifetime.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,500 - $3,500+ / year (If the original wiring harness shorts and fries the EZL module, repairs can exceed $2,000 immediately. Preventative replacement of the timing chain guides costs ~$700. Replacing plastic oil tubes with aluminum costs ~$500. A/C evaporator replacement is a massive dash-out job costing over $2,500.)"
