        compression: "9.0:1",
        yearRange: "2002-2006",
        timingType: "Single-roller chain. The M113K (Kompressor) features the same reliable single-row chain architecture as the naturally aspirated version, which is generally bulletproof. However, due to the significantly higher boost-induced torque and the heavy load of the belt-driven supercharger, the chain and tensioners are subjected to increased stress. Preventative replacement of the tensioner at 100,000 km is highly advisable to avoid any timing slack.",
        injectionType: "Multi-Point Injection / Bosch Motronic ME 2.8. This system uses a mass airflow sensor and is specifically tuned for the forced induction provided by the IHI supercharger. The system is robust, but it requires high-performance fuel pressure and clean injectors to manage the 500 hp output. Carbon buildup is less of an issue here than on later direct-injected engines, but proper fuel quality is essential to prevent detonation.",
        engineLifespan: "200 000 - 300 000+ km. The M113K is considered a modern performance legend. The block is virtually identical to the naturally aspirated M113, with forged internals specifically upgraded by AMG to handle the massive 0.8 bar boost from the factory supercharger. It is an extremely long-lived engine if the cooling system is kept pristine and the intercooler pump is functioning, though the increased boost significantly shortens the life of rubber seals and plastic components compared to the NA engine.",
        turboType: "Supercharged (IHI Lysholm-type twin-screw supercharger). The supercharger is belt-driven and provides instant, lag-free torque across the entire rev range. Its biggest weakness is the internal oil reservoir, which eventually runs dry if not serviced, leading to bearing failure, and the complex electromagnetic clutch assembly that engages the supercharger.",
        ecuType: "Bosch Motronic ME 2.8. This ECU is highly sophisticated and controls the supercharger engagement via an electromagnetic clutch. It also monitors the intercooler circuit temperature to pull boost if heat soak is detected. Tuning this ECU is popular for increasing boost, but it requires careful monitoring of the AFR (Air-Fuel Ratio) to avoid piston melting.",
        euroStandard: "Euro 4",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "600+ hp. The forged internals are incredibly over-engineered. Many tuners push these engines well beyond factory output with simple pulley swaps and ECU tunes. The block itself rarely fails, though the transmission and driveline components are often the first to give up under sustained maximum power.",
