    yearRange: "2013-2019",
    timingType: "Single-roller timing chain. The M270 utilizes a chain-driven valvetrain that is significantly more reliable than the older M271 architecture. However, high-mileage engines or those with stretched oil change intervals can suffer from timing chain stretch and VVT phaser wear. A brief rattling noise on cold starts is a common early indicator that the chain tensioner is failing to build pressure quickly enough.",
    injectionType: "Gasoline Direct Injection (GDI) / Bosch MED 17.7.2. Fuel is sprayed directly into the combustion chamber at extremely high pressure, ensuring excellent fuel economy and throttle response. Because fuel no longer washes over the intake valves, this engine is prone to carbon buildup over time, which requires specialized cleaning.",
    engineLifespan: "200 000 - 300 000+ km. The 1.6L M270 is a robust, well-designed four-cylinder engine. Because the CLA 180 variant is significantly detuned from the factory via software, the internal components are under very low mechanical stress. As long as the cooling system components are proactively replaced and oil changes are kept strictly to 10,000 km intervals, the engine block will easily outlast the car's interior and transmission.",
    turboType: "Single Turbocharger (IHI). Uses a relatively small, fast-spooling water-cooled turbocharger designed for quick low-end torque delivery in urban driving. The turbocharger itself is generally reliable, but the wastegate actuator linkage frequently develops play, causing an annoying rattle and eventual boost leaks.",
    ecuType: "Bosch MED 17.7.2. A highly reliable engine control unit. Because the CLA 180 and CLA 200 share the exact same 1.6L engine hardware, the 122 hp output of the CLA 180 is purely a software restriction mapped into this ECU. This makes the CLA 180 one of the most rewarding cars on the market for aftermarket tuning.",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "200+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and cast aluminum pistons are identical to those found in the 156 hp CLA 200. The engine safely handles Stage 1 and Stage 2 tuning, with the 7G-DCT transmission clutch packs usually reaching their thermal limits before the engine block does.",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "7.0 - 8.5 L/100km",
      highway: "4.8 - 6.0 L/100km",
      mixed: "5.5 - 6.8 L/100km",
      sporty: "9.5+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "5.8L 5W-30 or 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5 or MB 229.51). Frequent oil changes are essential to prevent the direct injection system from causing fuel dilution in the oil, which degrades turbocharger bearing lubrication.",
    commonIssues: [
      "Thermostat Housing Leaks: The factory thermostat housing is manufactured from plastic and is located in an area subjected to constant thermal cycling. Over time, the material becomes brittle, leading to hairline cracks and slow coolant leaks. If ignored, the leak can suddenly worsen, causing the engine to overheat rapidly in heavy traffic.",
      "Carbon Buildup on Intake Valves: Because the M270 relies on direct fuel injection, no fuel washes over the back of the intake valves to keep them clean. This leads to heavy carbon buildup over 100,000 km, restricting engine airflow and altering the air-fuel mixture. Symptoms include rough idling, hesitation under acceleration, and reduced fuel economy, requiring professional walnut blasting to cure.",
      "Wastegate Actuator Rattle: The linkage for the turbocharger wastegate actuator often develops play and wears down prematurely. This creates a distinct metallic rattling noise during deceleration or when revving the engine in neutral. While it rarely causes catastrophic failure immediately, it eventually leads to boost leaks and underboost engine codes.",
      "7G-DCT Mechatronics and Jerky Shifting: The 7-speed dual-clutch transmission is prone to software and mechanical valve body (mechatronic) issues. Drivers frequently experience jerky downshifts, harsh engagement from a standstill, and delayed reverse gear selection. Regular fluid changes strictly every 60,000 km are absolutely vital to mitigate these issues and preserve the delicate clutch packs.",
      "Water Pump Failure: The mechanically driven water pump uses a plastic impeller and seals that degrade due to constant exposure to high-temperature coolant. Bearings can fail, causing a whining noise, followed closely by coolant seeping from the weep hole. Preventative replacement at 100,000 km is highly recommended to avoid sudden overheating."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Overheating due to Plastic Cooling Components: The entire cooling system architecture relies heavily on plastic housings, flanges, and piping. When these components inevitably crack from age and heat, they can dump coolant instantly. Since the M270 block is entirely aluminum, driving even a short distance without coolant will quickly warp the cylinder head and blow the head gasket.",
      "DCT Clutch Pack Wear in City Driving: The wet clutches in the 7G-DCT transmission are highly stressed in stop-and-go city driving. Unlike a traditional fluid torque converter, crawling in heavy traffic causes the physical clutches to constantly slip, leading to accelerated friction material wear. Replacing a burned-out dual-clutch assembly is one of the most expensive repairs on the C117 platform.",
      "Software-Restricted Performance Safety Risk: While not a mechanical failure, the CLA 180 is severely detuned via software from the factory, making the car feel sluggish given its heavy curb weight and aggressive styling. The engine must work significantly harder and stay in higher RPMs to safely overtake on the highway, increasing fuel consumption and stress compared to an unrestricted tune.",
      "Timing Chain Stretch: Although far better than previous generations, the single-row timing chain can stretch over high mileage, especially if oil change intervals are neglected. A stretched chain alters engine timing, causes poor performance, and if the tensioner fails completely, the chain can jump teeth, causing catastrophic valve-to-piston contact.",
      "PCV Valve Failure: The crankcase ventilation (PCV) system is prone to clogging or internal diaphragm failure. When this happens, excess crankcase pressure forces oil into the intake tract and combustion chamber. This rapidly accelerates carbon buildup, fouls spark plugs, and significantly increases overall oil consumption."
    ],
