    timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC timing system. The M270 uses a front-mounted timing chain driving dual overhead camshafts with variable cam phasing, which is mechanically preferable to a rear-mounted layout because inspection and service access are far more realistic in chassis-installed conditions. In theory the chain is a long-life component, but in real ownership the service life depends heavily on oil quality, oil change frequency, and whether the engine has been repeatedly short-tripped on old, fuel-diluted oil. On neglected cars the main risk is progressive chain elongation together with guide and tensioner wear, which can begin with a cold-start rattle and then move into cam correlation faults, unstable valve timing, rough running, and expensive top-end work. A well-maintained example can run a very long time on the original chain, but this is not a 'fill-for-life and forget it' setup; regular listen-checks, fast oil service intervals, and immediate diagnosis of start-up rattles are the difference between normal ownership and a major repair.",
  
    injectionType: "Direct injection, turbocharged gasoline architecture. The M270 DE 16 AL family uses high-pressure direct fuel injection rather than conventional port injection, which improves combustion efficiency, torque delivery at low rpm, and emissions performance, especially in the compact transverse Mercedes platform. The trade-off is classic GDI behavior: intake valves do not receive the detergent washing effect of fuel spray, so oil vapor and blow-by deposits accumulate over time, gradually creating carbon buildup that can affect idle quality, cold-start smoothness, fuel economy, and airflow. The system also places more demand on fuel quality, injector condition, high-pressure pump health, and crankcase ventilation integrity. For long-term reliability, this engine rewards periodic intake cleaning strategy, quality fuel, correct oil approvals, and very quick action when misfire, fuel trim, or rough-idle symptoms appear.",
  
    engineLifespan: "220,000-320,000+ km is realistic when serviced correctly, with some engines capable of more if timing wear, cooling faults, and carbon buildup are addressed before secondary damage occurs. The aluminum block and modern turbo DI layout are not inherently weak, but this is not a neglect-tolerant engine; it depends on clean oil, a healthy PCV system, stable cooling control, and prompt attention to chain noise or oil leaks. In real-world ownership the engine's lifespan is usually limited less by the hard parts of the short block and more by accessory-system neglect, intake contamination, stretched service intervals, and owners who continue driving through early warning signs.",
  
    turboType: "Turbocharged, single twin-scroll turbocharger integrated for low-speed response and efficiency. On the 122 hp CLA 180 BlueEFFICIENCY calibration the turbo is tuned for torque availability, fuel economy, and drivability rather than outright top-end performance, so the engine feels stronger in the midrange than the power figure suggests. The hardware is generally durable if oil quality is maintained and the PCV/breather system stays healthy, but repeated hot shutdowns after heavy use, long oil intervals, contaminated oil feed, or intake leaks can shorten turbo bearing and wastegate life.",
  
    ecuType: "Bosch MED17-series engine management (application-dependent Mercedes calibration). The ECU controls direct injection, boost, electronic throttle, knock management, variable cam timing, and emissions strategy with fairly conservative factory mapping on the BlueEFFICIENCY Edition. Tuning potential exists, but this calibration is intentionally economy-biased and linked to drivetrain and thermal limits, so aggressive remaps without intake, charge-air, and maintenance preparation are not mechanically intelligent. A mild software calibration can sharpen response, but the real priority on this platform is condition correction before performance enhancement.",
  
    euroStandard: "Euro 6",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "170-190 hp is generally the sensible ceiling on healthy stock internals with conservative tuning and proper thermal management. The bottom end is not the immediate problem at this level; the limiting factors are heat control, turbo efficiency range, direct-injection cleanliness, and gearbox/clutch sympathy. Pushing beyond that is usually poor value on a CLA 180 BlueEFFICIENCY because the supporting hardware, economy gearing, and base-state calibration were never intended to mimic the stronger factory variants.",
  
