          timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC timing system using the same toothed timing-chain arrangement as the global 360 PS pre-facelift M133.980. The USA-spec rating difference is a measurement standard issue rather than a hardware downgrade, so mechanically the timing system faces the same high-output AMG operating environment. That means oil quality, warm-up discipline, and immediate response to cold-start noise remain essential. In neglected cars, the symptoms are the usual high-performance-turbo warning signs: brief start-up rattle, timing adaptation drift, roughness, and progressively more expensive top-end correction if ignored. The important point is that the 355 hp SAE version must be maintained exactly like the 360 PS version, because it is the same serious hand-built AMG engine underneath the rating format.",
      
          injectionType: "Spray-guided high-pressure direct injection with piezo-controlled injectors, identical in architecture to the non-USA pre-facelift M133.980. The 355 hp figure reflects SAE net output rather than a different fundamental fueling concept, so the same benefits and same liabilities apply: exceptionally precise combustion control, high knock resistance under boost, and strong torque density, but also inevitable intake-valve carbon accumulation over time because fuel never washes the valve backs. On an AMG engine that makes more than 175 hp per liter, injector quality, ignition integrity, and intake cleanliness all matter greatly. As mileage rises, maintaining premium fuel quality and planning for intake cleaning are not enthusiast extras; they are part of normal responsible ownership.",
      
          engineLifespan: "Expected lifespan is broadly the same as the global 360 PS engine, around 220,000-300,000+ km with excellent care. The SAE-rated 355 hp figure does not indicate a softer or more durable version; it is simply the U.S. way of stating output. The real determinants of longevity are service frequency, heat management, transmission maintenance, fuel quality, and whether the car has been modified or repeatedly launch-controlled without corresponding preventative work. A carefully maintained U.S.-spec CLA 45 can age very well, but a neglected one becomes expensive because every component in the system is operating in a high-performance environment.",
      
          turboType: "Single twin-scroll turbocharger with intercooling, same core high-performance configuration as the 360 PS pre-facelift variant. The turbo system gives the U.S.-spec CLA 45 the same hard midrange hit, rapid spool behavior, and forceful upper-range delivery expected from the M133. Because the power rating difference is only SAE versus DIN/PS convention, there is no real-world reduction in the engine's aggressiveness. As always, the system depends on excellent oil condition, charge-air sealing, and strong intercooling performance if it is to remain both fast and reliable.",
      
          ecuType: "AMG-specific Bosch MED17-series engine management with U.S.-market calibration and emissions compliance strategy. The ECU logic still aggressively manages boost, knock control, direct injection, throttle, and variable cam timing in a way that feels far sharper than ordinary Mercedes turbo engines. Tuning potential remains very strong, but the same warning applies as on the non-U.S. car: the factory map is already a highly developed performance calibration, so further gains only make sense after maintenance quality is fully established.",
      
