            timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC timing system using the same fundamental AMG valvetrain architecture as the global facelift M133.980. The important detail is that this U.S.-spec engine still shares the facelift-era higher-output operating behavior even though its published horsepower figure is 375 hp instead of 381 PS, because that difference is largely a standards and market-specification matter rather than a softer mechanical package. Timing reliability is therefore governed by the same rules: clean oil, short service intervals, proper warm-up, and immediate attention to cold-start rattle or adaptation drift. Because the engine code remained unchanged, many owners underestimate how much the facelift-era state of tune and emissions packaging still demand disciplined lubrication and diagnostics.",
        
            injectionType: "High-pressure spray-guided direct injection with facelift-era AMG control strategy and U.S.-market emissions calibration. The U.S. facelift cars retained the same core direct-injection benefits, namely excellent combustion precision, very strong torque density, and stable knock control under boost, but also the same structural drawback of intake valves receiving no fuel wash. The facelift U.S. spec is particularly interesting because the power figure is quoted differently while the engine is still fundamentally operating at the facelift M133 level, and in your note that is paired with modified catalyst construction as part of the market-specific setup. In practical maintenance terms, this means the engine still needs premium fuel, strong plugs and coils, and eventual intake cleaning to preserve the sharpness of the factory tune and keep emissions-related combustion behavior clean and stable.",
        
            engineLifespan: "Expected lifespan remains around 220,000-300,000+ km when maintained correctly, because the U.S. facelift engine is still built on the same hand-built forged M133 foundation as the 381 PS global car. The critical point is that the engine code did not change, yet the year-specific operating package did, so lifecycle expectations should follow the facelift logic rather than the early 355 hp U.S. profile. In real-world ownership, long-term survival depends on cooling stability, premium fuel quality, carbon management, ignition freshness, and DCT servicing just as much as oil changes. If those areas are respected, the engine can age impressively well. If they are not, the unchanged engine code can trick owners into assuming the car is simpler than it really is.",
        
            turboType: "Single twin-scroll turbocharger with facelift-era boost strategy and market-specific emissions integration. Even in U.S. 375 hp form, the engine delivers the stronger facelift torque figure of 475 Nm, and in real-world use it behaves like the harder-edged updated M133 rather than a mild variation of the old pre-facelift car. The turbo system is highly effective and brutally responsive for a 2.0-liter four-cylinder, but because the facelift package is already aggressive, any charge-air inefficiency, heat soak, or small leak has an immediate effect on how special the car feels.",
        
            ecuType: "AMG-specific Bosch MED17-series engine management with facelift U.S.-market calibration. This is another case where year matters far more than engine code alone, because the U.S. facelift car shares the stronger facelift operating concept while also carrying market-specific catalyst and emissions differences. The ECU therefore should not be thought of as a trivial regional variant; it is a key part of how the facelift engine delivers its stronger torque and updated behavior. Tuning remains highly effective, but as with all M133s, the smarter approach is to stabilize cooling, ignition, intake cleanliness, and transmission health before chasing additional software gains.",
        
            euroStandard: "U.S. market emissions specification",
            maxPowerStockInternals: "430-500 hp remains realistic on healthy stock internals with proper supporting hardware. The 375 hp SAE figure does not indicate a meaningfully weaker engine than the 381 PS facelift global car, so the same broad tuning ceiling applies. As always on the M133, the short block is not the first limitation; thermal control, charge-air temperature, ignition quality, and DCT condition determine whether higher output is usable and durable.",
        
