              oilSpecification: "MB-approved low-SAPS synthetic diesel oil, typically 5W-30 meeting Mercedes diesel particulate filter requirements",
          
              commonIssues: [
                "Timing belt neglect: The biggest strategic risk on the OM607 is owners ignoring belt interval history because the engine is seen as an economical commuter motor. If the belt, tensioner, and related service items are overdue, the engine immediately becomes a financial risk regardless of how nicely it drives.",
                "EGR valve and intake contamination: Soot accumulation from low-load urban diesel use can cause sticky EGR behavior, airflow disruption, hesitation, and gradual drivability deterioration. This is extremely common on cars that rarely see long, hot runs.",
                "DPF loading from short-trip use: The engine is economical, but it does not enjoy repeated cold urban use with interrupted regenerations. Eventually the car starts showing rising fuel consumption, fan overrun behavior, poor throttle response, and expensive emissions-system complaints.",
                "Injector correction drift: As mileage rises or fuel quality varies, one or more injectors may begin deviating in correction values. That can show up as rough idle, diesel knock variation, smoke, hard starting, or poor economy.",
                "Turbo actuator or boost-control contamination: Soot, oil vapor, and age can interfere with the variable-geometry turbo system, causing lazy response, underboost faults, or inconsistent torque delivery.",
                "Oil leaks and breather contamination: Small upper-engine leaks or crankcase ventilation issues can turn into intake contamination and grime buildup. While not always catastrophic, they matter on a compact diesel where intake cleanliness strongly influences overall behavior.",
                "Clutch and dual-mass flywheel wear on manual cars: The engine's low-end torque and economy-oriented driving style often encourage lugging. Over time this can create flywheel vibration, shudder, and expensive driveline wear."
              ],
          
              vulnerabilities: [
                "The main vulnerability of the OM607 is not that it is a bad engine, but that it is a very usage-sensitive small modern diesel. When used correctly, it can be durable, efficient, and impressively inexpensive to run. When forced into cold-start city life with delayed service, it quickly accumulates EGR, DPF, injector, and intake-related problems that erase all of its economy advantages.",
                "The Renault-Mercedes cooperation background creates a market-perception problem. Some buyers dismiss it unfairly because it is not a 'full Mercedes' diesel, while others assume that because the base engine is widely used, any example will be cheap to own. Both views are wrong; condition, belt history, and emissions-system health matter far more than badge politics.",
                "Timing belt history is an economic vulnerability that completely changes the purchase value of the car. A cheap OM607 CLA with undocumented belt service is often not cheap at all once proper preventive work is added immediately. This is why paperwork matters more than cosmetic condition.",
                "Urban use is especially punishing on this engine because the emissions hardware is integral to normal operation. A car that never reaches stable long-duration operating temperature gradually moves toward EGR fouling, poor regeneration, rising soot load, and eventually limp-mode style complaints. On this engine, usage pattern is part of maintenance.",
                "Low-end torque encourages mechanically lazy driving habits. Many owners shift early and load the engine heavily at low rpm, which feels economical but places extra stress on the clutch, dual-mass flywheel, turbo response zone, and combustion cleanliness. Driven properly, it is smooth; lugged constantly, it becomes expensive.",
                "Fuel quality matters more than many budget-minded diesel buyers want to admit. Poor diesel quality or infrequent filter service can worsen injector behavior, combustion noise, smoke, and cold starting. A cheap tank strategy often becomes a costly injector strategy later.",
                "Because the engine is modest in output, small losses are easy to ignore. A slightly sticky EGR, a partially loaded DPF, or mild boost-control issue may not make the car feel 'broken,' only slower and rougher than it should be. That causes many owners to delay diagnosis until several small issues have combined."
              ],
          
