  author: ".",
  popupAnimationName: "",
          
              drivingExperience: "The OM607 CLA 180 d feels exactly like what it is: an economy-led premium compact diesel with respectable low-end torque and excellent cruising efficiency. It is not quick, but it is easy to live with when used properly, and the 260 Nm torque figure gives it enough midrange flexibility to feel less strained than the modest horsepower suggests. Manual versions reward sensible gear choice and smooth diesel driving technique, while later automatic variants feel more polished in traffic. The overall experience is defined more by efficiency, range, and relaxed daily use than by performance.",
          
              mechanicVerdict: "The OM607.951 is a sensible engine when judged honestly and maintained correctly. It is not prestigious, and it is not especially exciting, but it can be very economical and durable. The two most important points are timing belt history and usage pattern. If the belt service is undocumented, I treat the engine as an immediate maintenance liability. If the car has lived an all-urban short-trip life, I assume EGR and DPF-related wear are already part of the picture until proven otherwise. The Renault-Mercedes cooperation background should not scare you by itself. What matters is whether the car was serviced properly with correct oil, filters, and diesel operating habits. Mechanically, the engine is strongest when used as intended: regular longer runs, complete warm-up, and calm but not lugged driving. The main vulnerabilities are belt neglect, emissions-system fouling, injector drift, and manual-driveline wear from low-rpm abuse. The best modifications are not performance parts. Do the timing belt properly. Keep oil intervals short. Maintain the fuel filter. Clean the intake and EGR before symptoms become severe. Use quality diesel. If you want a cheap-to-run CLA diesel and you understand how a modern small diesel wants to be used, this is a reasonable choice. If you want a neglect-tolerant city car, it is the wrong engine.",
          
              repairCostEstimate: "Approx. 700-1,500 EUR per year depending on mileage and whether major preventive work has already been done. Light years are inexpensive and mostly involve oil, filters, and routine inspection. Medium years often add fuel filter, EGR cleaning, glow plugs, or transmission service. Heavier years usually involve timing belt service, clutch/DMF work on manuals, or emissions-system intervention if the car has been used badly. The OM607 is affordable only when serviced proactively."
