              oilSpecification: "MB-approved low-SAPS synthetic diesel oil, typically 5W-30 suitable for Euro 6 DPF applications",
          
              commonIssues: [
                "DPF loading from short-trip fleet use: The OM622 is highly efficient, but it is still a Euro 6 diesel that dislikes interrupted regenerations and repeated cold urban cycles.",
                "EGR and intake contamination: Soot and oily vapor buildup gradually reduce airflow quality, increase roughness, and complicate regeneration behavior.",
                "Injector balance drift: Small fueling deviations can show up as rough idle, harder starting, combustion harshness, or inconsistent economy.",
                "Turbo and boost-control contamination: Even a mild small diesel turbo can suffer from soot-related control issues if use pattern and oil quality are poor.",
                "Cooling-system thermostat irregularity: Diesel warm-up quality matters for emissions behavior, and a lazy thermostat can create wide-reaching drivability and DPF side effects.",
                "Oil contamination from diesel operating pattern: Repeated cold starts and interrupted regeneration can degrade oil quality faster than many owners expect.",
                "Fleet maintenance shortcuts: Because this is a rare fleet-oriented variant, many examples may have seen pragmatic servicing rather than careful enthusiast ownership."
              ],
          
              vulnerabilities: [
                "The OM622's main vulnerability is that it looks mechanically sensible on paper but still lives in the real world as a small Euro 6 diesel. That means its ownership cost is controlled less by dramatic hardware weakness and more by regeneration quality, EGR cleanliness, thermostat behavior, and fuel/injector health. In other words, it is a system-sensitive engine rather than a brute-force one.",
                "Its rarity is a double-edged sword. On one hand, it can be an interesting and efficient choice in certain markets. On the other, rarity often means buyers know less about what they are looking at and sellers describe it vaguely. That makes documentation, diagnostics, and exact parts sourcing more important than on the more common OM607.",
                "Fleet usage pattern is a major concern. Many transitional Euro 6 diesel variants were bought on logic rather than enthusiasm, which usually means long intervals, low-budget fuel, and lots of urban usage. That does not destroy the engine automatically, but it dramatically changes how carefully you need to inspect the car.",
                "Because the output is modest, gradual deterioration is easy to normalize. A slightly sticky EGR, mediocre thermostat, or partially loaded DPF will not always create a dramatic failure straight away. Instead, the car slowly becomes rougher, less efficient, and less cooperative until multiple systems need attention together.",
                "Oil quality is more important than the low power figure suggests. Euro 6 regeneration behavior and soot control can degrade oil faster than owners expect, and poor oil quality affects timing-chain lubrication, turbo life, and overall combustion cleanliness. Cheap oil strategy on this engine is false economy.",
                "The engine is tuneable, but tuning is usually the wrong priority. On a healthy OM622 you are starting from a very efficient, emissions-conscious balance. Increasing torque without first proving DPF health, injector accuracy, and cooling stability simply shortens the useful life of the things that make the engine economical in the first place.",
                "Diagnosis quality matters because several small diesel problems can imitate each other. Poor regeneration, thermostat weakness, minor injector imbalance, and EGR fouling can all overlap. That means sloppy troubleshooting gets expensive quickly, especially on a rarer variant."
              ],
          
