              yearRange: "European fleet detuned series",
          
              timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC timing system. Like the stronger version, this 122 hp fleet-calibrated M282 uses a timing chain rather than a service-interval belt, which is a positive from both engineering and ownership perspectives. However, the lower output tune does not magically reduce the importance of lubrication quality; the hydraulic tensioner, variable cam timing hardware, and top-end oil passages still depend on regular clean-oil servicing to stay quiet and stable. Because many fleet-type cars are maintained to cost targets rather than enthusiast standards, this engine is especially vulnerable to extended oil intervals in real life. The hardware is fundamentally sound, but the ownership profile of this variant makes documentation and service discipline more important than on a privately cherished example.",
          
              injectionType: "Direct injection turbocharged petrol system with conservative software calibration. Mechanically this engine shares the same advanced DI architecture as the 136 hp version, but in this fleet-oriented state of tune it is electronically softened to meet taxation or operating-cost priorities. That means the same benefits remain—good low-rpm torque for size, efficient combustion, and compact packaging—but so do the same long-term DI realities, especially intake valve carbon buildup and strong dependence on clean combustion and ventilation control. The lower power output does not make the system simpler or more tolerant of neglect. It is still a modern direct-injection turbo engine and should be maintained accordingly.",
          
              engineLifespan: "250,000-320,000+ km is possible when the car has documented servicing and has not spent its life on long intervals, poor fuel, and endless short trips. The fleet tune does reduce mechanical stress slightly in everyday operation, but long-term life is still governed by the same factors as the stronger version: oil quality, cooling stability, intake cleanliness, ignition health, and boost-system integrity. In real-world buying, the largest risk is not the engine design itself but the possibility that a fleet car was maintained only to minimum contract standards. A well-kept example can last very well; a cost-managed example can age deceptively fast.",
          
              turboType: "Single turbocharger with conservative boost strategy. In this detuned configuration the turbo is asked to deliver smooth, efficient everyday performance rather than sporty response, which helps drivability and may marginally reduce stress compared with more aggressive calibrations. Even so, the turbo still depends on the same fundamentals: clean oil, sound charge-air plumbing, and healthy thermal management. Since the car is not very fast to begin with, small boost leaks or mild intake contamination are easy to ignore, and that is exactly why fleet variants often feel merely 'average' rather than obviously faulty while gradually drifting away from factory behavior.",
          
              ecuType: "Mercedes/Bosch direct-injection turbo ECU with software detune for fleet/tax positioning. The management remains fully modern, controlling boost, timing, direct injection, knock response, and emissions behavior, but the requested output and mapping strategy are reduced relative to the more energetic 136 hp tune. This makes the engine calmer, but it does not eliminate the need for excellent baseline maintenance. In fact, software-detuned fleet engines are often the ones most likely to be neglected because drivers assume the low output makes them nearly stress-free.",
          
