                oilSpecification: "MB 229.71 / MB 229.72 / MB 229.81",
            
                commonIssues: [
                  "Cooling-system precision faults: A modern high-efficiency turbo engine depends heavily on exact thermal control. Even a small thermostat drift, coolant seep, or control-valve problem can create slow warm-up, unstable operating temperature, reduced efficiency, and long-term stress on plastic cooling parts and turbo hardware.",
                  "Direct-injection intake carbon buildup: Because the intake valves are not washed by fuel, deposits accumulate over time. This eventually causes rough cold starts, less stable idle quality, muted throttle sharpness, and a gradual airflow loss that many owners first mistake for poor fuel or weak ignition.",
                  "Charge-air leaks and underboost behavior: Small leaks in intercooler pipe joints, seals, or couplers can flatten response noticeably. On a refined mild-hybrid setup the electric support can partially mask this, which means the mechanical loss often develops quietly before it becomes obvious.",
                  "Ignition sensitivity under load: Worn plugs or a weakening coil can create soft hesitation, partial misfire, and inconsistent torque under boost. Small modern turbo engines usually show this under warm acceleration or mid-range load before they show obvious idle complaints.",
                  "PCV / crankcase ventilation degradation: Once the breather side starts losing efficiency, oil mist contamination rises, intake deposits form faster, and idle quality can deteriorate. It also complicates leak diagnosis because secondary symptoms begin appearing across several systems at once.",
                  "GPF efficiency loss on repeated short trips: Constant cold urban usage can compromise particulate-filter operating conditions over time. This generally shows up as muted response, slightly poorer consumption, and increased sensitivity to low-load driving cycles rather than a dramatic immediate failure.",
                  "Extended oil interval damage to chain and cam-control hardware: The chain is a strength only when the lubricant strategy is sensible. Over-long service intervals accelerate wear in the tensioner, cam phasers, and upper valvetrain hydraulics, especially on a downsized turbo engine with frequent start-stop operation."
                ],
            
                vulnerabilities: [
                  "The main vulnerability is system complexity concentration. The engine combines direct injection, turbocharging, particulate filtering, advanced combustion strategy, and 48V hybrid support in one compact package. That does not make it weak, but it means one neglected maintenance area can begin affecting several other systems quickly.",
                  "Thermal-management negligence is economically dangerous. A car can still drive well enough with a mild thermostat problem or coolant seep, which often encourages owners to postpone the repair. On this type of engine that delay increases long-term stress on the turbocharger, plastic housings, seals, and internal efficiency.",
                  "Intake cleanliness will eventually become a lifecycle issue. The engine can remain smooth for quite a long time while deposits gradually build on the valves, which is why many owners replace coils or plugs first without addressing the root airflow loss. Preventive cleaning is cheaper than chasing symptoms one by one.",
                  "The mild-hybrid system adds refinement, but it also adds dependency. When the hybrid coordination, transmission behavior, or 48V support loses sharpness, the car feels less polished even if the combustion engine is still mechanically healthy. Proper diagnosis therefore matters more than guesswork.",
                  "Small losses are easy to ignore because the powertrain is naturally smooth. A soft boost leak, early ignition weakness, or slight PCV deterioration may only make the car feel a little duller rather than obviously broken. That delayed symptom curve is exactly what allows medium-cost problems to stack up.",
                  "Cheap tuning is a real technical risk on this engine. The hardware can respond to a software change, but extra load on top of old plugs, marginal cooling control, dirty intake valves, or minor boost leakage simply accelerates wear and pushes diagnostics into a much more expensive zone."
                ],
            
