                timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC timing system. The M282 uses a front-mounted timing chain instead of a scheduled belt, which is a meaningful long-term ownership advantage on a compact turbo petrol engine. However, the chain system is still sensitive to lubricant condition, fuel dilution, and cold-start wear, especially on cars that spent their life on stretched service intervals or low-budget fleet maintenance. If oil changes are delayed, chain elongation, tensioner wear, and cam-control irregularity can begin appearing quietly. In other words, this is a durable chain setup when maintained correctly, but not a mechanical excuse for neglect.",
            
                injectionType: "Turbocharged direct injection with a conservative low-output market-specific calibration. The engine uses modern GDI combustion to deliver acceptable low-rpm torque and fuel efficiency from a very small displacement, but the same direct-injection side effects remain present even in this detuned version. Intake valves are not cleaned by fuel, so carbon buildup still accumulates over time and can eventually affect idle quality, cold-start smoothness, and airflow consistency. The lower power output does not make the engine simpler; it only hides some problems for longer.",
            
                engineLifespan: "A realistic service life is 240,000-320,000+ km if the engine has documented oil service, stable cooling behavior, and no long-standing boost or ignition issues. The low-output tune reduces mechanical stress in standard use, but the engine still lives or dies by the same rules as any modern small turbo DI unit. Oil quality, thermostat health, deposit control, and honest maintenance history matter more than the modest factory power figure. In practice, this engine often survives or fails according to ownership quality rather than hardware limits.",
            
                turboType: "Single turbocharger with conservative boost demand and economy-oriented calibration. In the CLA 160 the turbo is there primarily to give acceptable low-speed flexibility and daily drivability rather than exciting acceleration, which means the car feels usable in traffic despite the modest power rating. The positive side is relatively low hardware stress in stock form. The negative side is that minor boost leaks, dirty intake valves, or soft ignition problems can hide for a long time because the baseline performance is already mild.",
            
