                oilSpecification: "MB 229.71 (0W-20) or MB 229.51 (5W-30) Synthetic",
            
                commonIssues: [
                  "Fleet Maintenance Neglect: Because these models were frequently used as corporate lease vehicles, they often suffered from extended 20,000+ km oil change intervals. This severely degrades the oil, leading to accelerated timing chain wear and turbo bearing scoring.",
                  "Intake Valve Carbon Buildup: Due to the direct-injection design, severe carbon accumulation restricts airflow, causing cold-start misfires, rough idle, and sluggish throttle response.",
                  "Thermal Management Module Leaks: The plastic coolant distribution module often develops internal cracks or seal failures, leaking coolant into the electrical connectors or dripping externally.",
                  "Ignition Coil Failure: The engine is highly sensitive to ignition health. Aging coils will cause pronounced hesitation and misfires under heavy load.",
                  "PCV Valve / Valve Cover Failure: The integrated PCV membrane inside the plastic valve cover tears over time, creating a massive vacuum leak, a high-pitched whistling sound, and rough idling.",
                  "Thermostat Sticking: Electronic thermostats can fail in the open position, preventing the engine from reaching optimal operating temperature and decreasing fuel efficiency."
                ],
            
                vulnerabilities: [
                  "Coolant Migration via Wiring Harness: If the thermal management module fails internally, pressurized coolant can be pushed through the electrical connector and wick up the wiring harness directly into the ECU. This causes catastrophic electrical failure requiring a new ECU and wiring loom.",
                  "Chain Stretch from Oil Neglect: Relying on the factory 20,000+ km extended service intervals heavily degrades the oil, accelerating wear on the timing chain guides and hydraulic tensioner. A stretched chain can jump timing, causing piston-to-valve contact.",
                  "Turbocharger Bearing Coking: Shutting the engine down immediately after hard driving causes the oil inside the hot turbocharger to coke (burn and solidify). This destroys the turbo bearings over time.",
                  "Plastic Charge Pipe Cracking: The pressurized pipes connecting the turbo to the intercooler and throttle body become brittle with heat cycles. A cracked pipe causes a sudden and complete loss of boost pressure.",
                  "Oil Dilution from Short Trips: Frequent short trips prevent the engine from reaching operating temperature, causing unburnt fuel to bypass the piston rings and dilute the oil, destroying its lubricating properties.",
                  "Dual-Clutch Fluid Neglect: Fleet drivers rarely report transmission shudder. If the 7G-DCT misses its 60,000 km fluid change, the mechatronics unit and clutch packs will degrade rapidly, leading to massively expensive transmission overhauls."
                ],
            
