                timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC timing system with variable camshaft timing. The chain hardware remains fundamentally strong, but mild-hybrid operation increases restart frequency and places greater importance on flawless lubrication during repeated engine stop-start cycles. That means oil condition matters even more here than on the conventional version, because the chain, cam phasers, and hydraulic tensioning all benefit from quick, clean oil pressure on every restart event. When serviced on short intervals, the timing setup is durable; when treated to stretched intervals, the hybrid smoothness can mask early wear until cold-start noise and timing adaptation drift are already present.",
            
                injectionType: "High-pressure direct injection combined with mild-hybrid torque support. The combustion side of the engine behaves like any other modern Mercedes GDI turbo unit: efficient, torquey, and prone to intake valve carbon buildup because no fuel washes the valve backs. The electrical assist can make the car feel smoother and fuller at low rpm, which sometimes delays driver awareness that the combustion engine is losing airflow quality due to deposits. That makes scheduled inspection, fuel-trim monitoring, and eventual intake cleaning especially important, because the hybrid support can hide the early phase of GDI aging.",
            
                engineLifespan: "The mechanical engine itself can realistically cover 250,000-320,000+ km if oil service, cooling, and intake cleanliness are managed properly. The bigger long-term question is not the short block but the electrified support ecosystem: the 48V system, starter-generator-related hardware, control integration, and the added diagnostic complexity that comes with them. In other words, the engine core is strong, but total powertrain ownership life depends on how gracefully the electrical and thermal support systems age around it.",
            
                turboType: "Single twin-scroll turbocharger working alongside mild-hybrid torque fill. This arrangement improves responsiveness by letting electric assist cover the low-rpm phase while the turbo builds boost, producing a smoother and more immediate feel than a purely combustion setup of similar output. The turbo itself still depends on correct oil quality, clean charge-air plumbing, stable coolant management, and sensible heat management after hard driving. Hybrid assistance improves drivability, but it does not reduce the turbo's maintenance sensitivity.",
            
                ecuType: "Bosch MED17-series combustion ECU integrated with mild-hybrid control logic and drivetrain torque management. Unlike a simple turbo gasoline engine, this setup must coordinate throttle request, boost target, ignition strategy, electrical assist, and gearbox behavior in a way that feels seamless to the driver. That complexity makes diagnosis more demanding and tuning more delicate, because a problem that feels like engine hesitation can actually be a torque-coordination issue between combustion and hybrid systems.",
            
