                timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC timing system located at the front of the engine. The M260 utilizes a highly durable roller chain that theoretically lasts the lifetime of the vehicle under perfect conditions. However, the European standard of long flexible oil intervals (up to 25,000 km) completely destroys the hydraulic chain tensioners over time, causing cold-start rattling and eventual timing jump. Proactive 10,000 km oil changes are absolutely critical to maintaining hydraulic pressure and preventing catastrophic chain stretch.",
            
                injectionType: "High-pressure Direct Injection (GDI) utilizing central piezo injectors operating up to 200 bar. This setup optimizes fuel atomization and combustion chamber cooling, allowing for a high 10.0:1 compression ratio under boost. Because the fuel bypasses the intake tract entirely, the intake valves do not get washed, guaranteeing severe carbon buildup by 80,000 km which necessitates professional physical cleaning to restore lost airflow and idle quality.",
            
                engineLifespan: "250,000+ km for the mechanical short block, but heavily dictated by the health of the cylinder head and cooling system. Early production M260 engines are plagued by a severe metallurgy defect in the exhaust valve seats, leading to compression loss and requiring a full cylinder head replacement. If the cylinder head is a revised unit and the owner adheres to extreme maintenance discipline, the forged crankshaft and closed-deck block will easily outlast the chassis.",
            
                turboType: "Single Twin-Scroll Turbocharger. The twin-scroll manifold separates exhaust pulses to drastically reduce turbo lag, allowing the engine to hit peak 350 Nm of torque at an incredibly low 1,800 RPM. It employs an ultra-precise electronic wastegate actuator, though this actuator can become prone to rattling or binding if subjected to extreme heat cycling and neglected cooling system maintenance.",
            
                ecuType: "Bosch MED 17.7.7 engine management. This highly sophisticated multi-core ECU manages everything from the variable valve lift (CAMTRONIC) to the direct injection pulses and electronic wastegate. It is highly receptive to aftermarket tuning, easily unlocking an additional 40-50 horsepower, but altering the torque limiters requires careful synchronization with the 7G-DCT transmission control unit (TCU).",
            
                euroStandard: "Euro 6d-TEMP",
                maxPowerStockInternals: "320 - 350 hp. The M260 block is exceptionally robust, sharing significant architectural DNA with the high-output AMG M139 engine (though lacking its extreme cooling and rotating assembly). A Stage 1 tune easily maxes out the turbocharger's efficiency map long before the connecting rods or pistons reach their mechanical breaking point.",
            
