                timingType: "Chain-driven DOHC timing system located at the rear of the engine (transmission side). The M254 represents a massive architectural shift from the M260, moving the timing drive to the rear to accommodate the Integrated Starter Generator (ISG). While the chain itself is highly robust, rear-mounted timing means any eventual chain or tensioner replacement requires full engine and transmission removal. Strict 10,000 km oil changes are non-negotiable to protect the hydraulic tensioners and delay this incredibly labor-intensive job.",
            
                injectionType: "High-pressure Direct Injection (GDI) utilizing advanced central piezo injectors operating at up to 350 bar. This extreme fuel pressure allows for incredibly precise multi-spark and multi-injection events per combustion cycle, drastically reducing emissions. However, the complete lack of port fuel wash means intake valve carbon buildup remains a certainty, necessitating professional physical cleaning (walnut blasting) around the 80,000 km mark.",
            
                engineLifespan: "250,000+ km for the mechanical short block, but the economic lifespan is heavily dictated by the complex 48V ISG ecosystem. Mercedes engineered the M254 with a newly designed cylinder head, completely resolving the catastrophic exhaust valve seat failure that plagued the older M260. The block utilizes NANOSLIDE cylinder coating and CONICSHAPE (trumpet-honing) to minimize friction. If the 48V electrical components survive, the physical internal combustion engine is exceptionally durable.",
            
                turboType: "Single Twin-Scroll Turbocharger engineered in collaboration with the AMG Petronas Formula 1 team technology. It features a segment-leading segmented exhaust manifold and ultra-precise electronic wastegate to virtually eliminate lag. The turbocharger operates in seamless conjunction with the ISG, providing an incredibly linear, naturally aspirated feeling of power delivery.",
            
                ecuType: "Bosch MED 17.7.7 / Advanced hybrid management network. The ECU oversees a mind-boggling amount of data, constantly transitioning power delivery between the internal combustion engine and the transmission-mounted ISG. Tuning this platform is extremely difficult; altering boost parameters without perfectly recalibrating the ISG torque-fill requests will instantly trigger a hybrid system limp mode.",
            
