                yearRange: "2019-2023",
            
                timingType: "The M260 utilizes a front-mounted, chain-driven DOHC timing system. Unlike the newer M254, the chain is accessible without removing the entire engine, significantly reducing labor costs for timing jobs. However, the chain guides and hydraulic tensioners remain highly sensitive to oil quality, necessitating strict 10,000 km oil change intervals to prevent premature stretching and rattling.",
            
                injectionType: "Equipped with high-pressure Direct Injection (GDI) utilizing advanced piezo injectors. While this ensures precise fuel metering and excellent power delivery, it entirely deprives the intake valves of fuel wash. As a result, carbon sludge rapidly accumulates on the intake ports and valves, requiring physical walnut blasting every 80,000 km to maintain optimal airflow and prevent cold-start misfires.",
            
                engineLifespan: "The physical block is exceptionally strong, featuring a closed-deck aluminum design and forged internals capable of exceeding 200,000 km. However, the economic lifespan is completely overshadowed by the infamous exhaust valve seat manufacturing defect. If the cylinder head has not been replaced with the revised part, premature valve failure is almost guaranteed to occur before 100,000 km, requiring a massive engine-out rebuild.",
            
                turboType: "Features a highly responsive twin-scroll turbocharger equipped with an electronic wastegate actuator. The twin-scroll design separates the exhaust pulses from alternating cylinders, virtually eliminating turbo lag and providing the punchy 400 Nm of torque low in the rev range. Over time, the electronic wastegate linkage can develop play, leading to underboost faults.",
            
                ecuType: "Governed by the sophisticated Bosch MED 17.7.7 engine management system. It provides aggressive AMG-specific mapping, orchestrating the rapid pops and bangs on upshifts. The ECU is highly tunable via OBD flashing, making it very popular in the aftermarket, though pushing the power drastically increases the thermal stress on the already vulnerable cylinder head.",
            
                euroStandard: "Euro 6d-TEMP / Euro 6d",
                maxPowerStockInternals: "380 - 400 hp on stock internals. The closed-deck block and forged rotating assembly can handle significant boost increases effortlessly. A simple Stage 1 software calibration can unlock up to 350 hp safely, transforming the CLA 35 into a genuinely fast sports car, provided the cylinder head defect has already been resolved.",
            
