                valves: 16,
                compression: "9.0:1",
                yearRange: "2019-present",
            
                timingType: "The M139 utilizes a heavy-duty, chain-driven DOHC timing system located on the right side of the engine block. Because the engine revs extremely fast and handles immense cylinder pressures, the timing chain and its hydraulic tensioners are subjected to exceptional mechanical loads. The system's longevity is completely dependent on strictly adhering to the 10,000 km oil change intervals using the specific AMG-approved 0W-40 viscosity, preventing chain elongation and tensioner failure.",
            
                injectionType: "Equipped with a highly sophisticated dual-injection system combining both high-pressure direct injection (piezo injectors) and manifold port injection (solenoid injectors). This dual setup optimizes fuel mixture at high RPMs while completely curing the carbon buildup plague that destroyed the intake valves of previous AMG models. The port injectors actively wash the intake valves with raw fuel during low-load driving, ensuring the intake tract remains pristine without the need for periodic walnut blasting.",
            
                engineLifespan: "The physical architecture is incredibly robust, featuring a chill-cast, closed-deck aluminum block that operates more like a race engine than a traditional road car motor. With religious fluid maintenance and proper warm-up/cool-down procedures, the core engine block will comfortably exceed 200,000 km. However, the external accessories—particularly the turbocharger cooling lines, electric water pumps, and high-pressure fuel pumps—will require replacement far earlier due to the immense heat generated.",
            
                turboType: "Features a massive twin-scroll turbocharger equipped with anti-friction roller bearings, allowing the compressor wheel to spin up to 169,000 RPM with minimal mechanical resistance. Unlike conventional setups, AMG mounted this turbocharger at the rear of the engine block, directly against the firewall, to optimize aerodynamic packaging and intake airflow. This rear-mounted configuration necessitates extensive heat shielding and an elaborate electric cooling system to prevent the turbo bearings from cooking after the engine is shut off.",
            
                ecuType: "Controlled by the incredibly complex Bosch MED 17.7.7 engine management system, which orchestrates everything from the dual-injection phasing to the electronic wastegate and split-cooling circuits. The ECU heavily monitors exhaust gas temperatures and torque limits to protect the sophisticated AMG Performance 4MATIC+ drivetrain. Tuning this ECU unlocks massive power but requires careful calibration to avoid overwhelming the stock cooling capacity and triggering factory torque limiters.",
            
                euroStandard: "Euro 6d-ISC-FCM",
                maxPowerStockInternals: "480 - 500 hp on stock internals. Because the base M139 shares its forged rotating assembly and chill-cast block with the 421 hp 'S' variant, the 387 hp model is massively under-stressed from the factory. A simple software calibration easily bridges the gap to the 'S' model and pushes well beyond 450 hp without risking structural block failure, provided high-octane 98+ RON fuel is utilized.",
            
                fuelConsumption: {
                  city: "11.5 - 12.5 L/100km",
                  highway: "7.5 - 8.5 L/100km",
                  mixed: "8.5 - 9.5 L/100km",
                  sporty: "16.0 - 20.0 L/100km"
                },
            
                oilSpecification: "MB 229.51 / MB 229.52 (0W-40 Synthetic). The 0W-40 weight is mandatory for providing instant lubrication to the roller-bearing turbocharger during cold starts while surviving 120°C+ oil temperatures on track.",
            
                commonIssues: [
                  "Turbocharger Cooling Line Leaks: The coolant and oil feed lines running to the rear-mounted turbocharger are subjected to extreme radiant heat. Over time, the O-rings harden and weep fluid down the back of the engine block.",
                  "Electronic Wastegate Actuator Failure: The high temperatures against the firewall often degrade the electronics within the turbocharger's wastegate actuator, leading to sudden underboost conditions and limp mode.",
                  "Spark Plug Electrode Erosion: Running peak boost pressures exceeding 2.0 bar causes the spark plugs to physically erode much faster than normal engines, causing severe misfires under heavy acceleration if not replaced strictly every 30,000 km.",
                  "Rear Differential Fluid Degradation: The AMG Torque Control rear differential utilizes two multi-disc clutches that aggressively vector torque. Hard driving quickly contaminates the differential fluid, leading to grinding noises during tight cornering.",
                  "High Oil Consumption During Break-in: The forged pistons and Nanoslide cylinder coatings require a lengthy and strict break-in period. Improperly broken-in M139 engines are notorious for consuming up to 1 liter of oil per 2,000 km.",
                  "Exhaust Valve Rattle: The electronically controlled exhaust flaps frequently seize or rattle due to carbon accumulation and extreme heat cycling, requiring lubrication or replacement of the actuator spring."
                ],
            
                vulnerabilities: [
                  "Rear-Mounted Turbo Thermal Stress: Placing the turbine against the firewall creates a localized heat pocket that bakes nearby wiring harnesses, vacuum lines, and cooling hoses. If the post-shutdown electric cooling fan fails, the turbo bearings will instantly cook in stagnant oil.",
                  "Split-Cooling System Complexity: The engine uses separate cooling circuits for the cylinder head and the engine block, utilizing both mechanical and electric water pumps. A failure in any one of these interconnected pumps will result in catastrophic, localized overheating that the dashboard gauge might not immediately display.",
                  "GPF (Gasoline Particulate Filter) Clogging: European models are equipped with restrictive particulate filters in the exhaust. Frequent short trips prevent the exhaust from reaching the extreme temperatures required to burn off soot, resulting in clogged filters and forced limp modes.",
                  "8G-DCT Wet Clutch Glazing: The new 8-speed dual-clutch transmission is incredibly fast but heavily stressed by the engine's launch control system. Repeated back-to-back launches will permanently glaze the wet clutches, requiring a staggeringly expensive transmission rebuild.",
                  "Nanoslide Cylinder Scoring: The iron-carbon cylinder wall coating is incredibly hard but ultra-thin. If a failed fuel injector washes the oil film away, or if dirty oil is circulated, the Nanoslide coating will score, necessitating a complete block replacement as it cannot be machined.",
                  "Torque Vectoring Differential Failure: The dual-clutch rear axle is a masterpiece of handling, but neglecting the strict fluid change intervals will cause the clutch packs to disintegrate, leading to total failure of the 4MATIC+ system."
                ],
            
                oemPlusUpgrades: [
                  { name: "Heat-Shielded Turbo Lines", description: "Wrapping or replacing the factory turbocharger coolant and oil lines with heavily shielded aftermarket variants.", benefit: "Prevents the lines from baking against the firewall, eliminating the most common source of fluid leaks on the M139." },
                  { name: "High-Performance 0W-40 Regiment", description: "Switching to Motul 300V or Liqui Moly 0W-40 and changing the oil every 8,000 km.", benefit: "Absolutely critical for maintaining the extremely tight tolerances of the roller-bearing turbocharger and preventing timing chain wear." },
                  { name: "Stage 1 ECU Calibration", description: "Flashing the Bosch MED 17.7.7 engine control unit to optimize boost and fueling curves.", benefit: "Safely bridges the artificial gap to the 'S' variant, unlocking roughly 420-440 horsepower without requiring hardware modifications." },
                  { name: "Upgraded High-Pressure Fuel Pump", description: "Installing a larger bore HPFP from an aftermarket supplier like Spool Performance.", benefit: "Provides the necessary fuel flow safety margin when running higher boost pressures, protecting against lean-out conditions." }
                ],
            
