                yearRange: "2019-present",
            
                timingType: "The M139 utilizes a heavy-duty, chain-driven DOHC timing system located on the right side of the engine block. Because the 'S' variant handles immense 2.1 bar peak boost cylinder pressures, the timing chain and its hydraulic tensioners are subjected to exceptional mechanical loads. The system's longevity is completely dependent on strictly adhering to the 10,000 km oil change intervals using the specific AMG-approved 0W-40 viscosity, which prevents rapid chain elongation and hydraulic tensioner collapse.",
            
                injectionType: "Equipped with a highly sophisticated dual-injection system combining high-pressure direct injection (piezo injectors) and manifold port injection (solenoid injectors). This dual setup optimizes fuel mixture at high RPMs to feed the 421 hp demand while completely curing the carbon buildup plague of previous AMG models. The port injectors actively wash the intake valves with raw fuel during low-load driving, ensuring the intake tract remains pristine without the need for periodic walnut blasting.",
            
                engineLifespan: "The physical architecture is incredibly robust, featuring a chill-cast, closed-deck aluminum block that operates more like a race engine than a road car motor. With religious fluid maintenance and proper warm-up/cool-down procedures, the core block will comfortably exceed 200,000 km. However, the external accessories—particularly the turbocharger cooling lines, 48V electrical components, and electric water pumps—will require replacement far earlier due to the immense heat generated by the rear-mounted exhaust.",
            
                turboType: "Features a massive twin-scroll turbocharger equipped with anti-friction roller bearings, allowing the compressor wheel to spin up to 169,000 RPM with minimal mechanical resistance to push 2.1 bar of boost. AMG mounted this turbocharger at the rear of the engine block, directly against the firewall, to optimize aerodynamic packaging and intake airflow. This configuration necessitates extensive heat shielding and an elaborate electric cooling system to prevent the turbo bearings from cooking after shutdown.",
            
                ecuType: "Controlled by the incredibly complex Bosch MED 17.7.7 engine management system, integrated with the new 48V mild-hybrid architecture on facelift models. The ECU orchestrates the dual-injection phasing, the electronic wastegate, split-cooling circuits, and the 14 hp Belt-Driven Starter Generator (RSG). Tuning this ECU unlocks massive power but requires careful calibration to avoid overwhelming the stock cooling capacity and triggering factory torque limiters protecting the DCT.",
            
                euroStandard: "Euro 6d-ISC-FCM",
                maxPowerStockInternals: "500 - 550 hp on stock internals. The M139 block is notoriously over-engineered, featuring forged aluminum pistons, a forged steel crankshaft, and Mercedes' patented Nanoslide cylinder coating. A simple Stage 1 software calibration easily pushes the 'S' model well beyond 460 hp, while Stage 2 hardware modifications can push it past 500 hp without risking structural block failure.",
            
