                timingType: "Timing Chain system, heavily integrated into the REAR of the engine block (flywheel side). This transverse layout utilizes a highly complex timing chain setup driving the dual overhead camshafts. While designed by Mercedes-Benz as a 'lifetime' component, delayed oil changes or excessive stop-start urban driving will rapidly cause the chain to stretch and the hydraulic tensioners to fail. Because the chain is located at the back, flush against the firewall, replacing a rattling timing chain requires dropping the entire transmission and subframe, resulting in staggering labor costs.",
            
                injectionType: "Ultra-high-pressure Common Rail Direct Injection (Bosch) operating at up to 2,050 bar. This OM654 generation features highly advanced piezo injectors capable of multiple micro-injections per combustion stroke, which almost entirely eliminates traditional diesel 'clatter'. The system is incredibly reliable but absolutely intolerant of poor-quality fuel, water contamination, or delayed fuel filter replacements. The high-pressure fuel pump (HPFP) relies purely on the lubricity of diesel fuel to survive, meaning any contamination can cause catastrophic metallic shrapnel to spread throughout the fuel system.",
            
                engineLifespan: "350,000+ km. The OM654 is a true, purebred Mercedes-Benz masterpiece featuring an all-aluminum block paired with stepped-bowl steel pistons and Nanoslide cylinder wall coatings. This material combination virtually eliminates the friction and thermal expansion issues of older designs, making the core block virtually indestructible. However, the true lifespan of the vehicle is usually dictated by the staggering cost of replacing the rear-mounted timing chain or servicing the incredibly complex dual-EGR and SCR emissions systems at high mileage.",
            
                turboType: "Equipped with a highly responsive Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VGT). The OM654 features water-cooled bearing housings on the turbocharger, drastically improving thermal stability and preventing oil coking after hot shutdowns. It spools almost instantly, providing the full 320 Nm of torque incredibly low in the RPM range. It is highly robust if the air filter is kept clean and the ultra-thin 0W-20 oil is changed frequently to ensure proper bearing lubrication.",
            
                ecuType: "Bosch MD1 Engine Management. The ECU controls an incredibly complex exhaust aftertreatment sequence, utilizing both High-Pressure and Low-Pressure EGR circuits, an SCR-coated DPF (sDPF), and an underbody SCR catalyst. This specific 136 hp 'Fleet' model is artificially restricted by the ECU to meet strict Belgian and European corporate tax brackets, despite sharing the exact identical physical hardware as the 150 hp and 190 hp variants.",
            
